<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vierlande</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vierlande (&quot;four lands&quot;) is a fertile marshland district on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, named after its four parishes. Pledged by the dukes of Saxe-Lauenburg, it was taken in 1420 by Hamburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, which governed it jointly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergedorf">Bergedorf</a> until 1868, when it passed wholly to Hamburg. It is known for market gardening of vegetables and flowers and for its distinctive farmhouses and costume.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vierlande.jpg" alt="Vierlande" width="320" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Pincerno at German Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pincerno_-_Vierlande_11-2008.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
